---
title: Any-vegetable stir-fry
description: Lesson 1.4. The module together, with ingredients you choose.
servings: 2
time: 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, heat-in-a-pan]
lesson: "1.4"
technique: stir-frying
status: draft
---

> Technique: stir-frying. This lesson uses the whole module: a hot pan
> (1.3), water as the thing that stops browning (1.2), and leaving food
> alone until it browns (1.1). The ingredients are slots: pick what you
> have for each one and keep the amounts.

Cut @protein{300%g}(chicken thigh, beef, pork, prawns or firm tofu) into bite-size pieces. Do the same with @hard vegetables{200%g}(carrot, broccoli, green beans), @soft vegetables{200%g}(peppers, mushrooms, courgette, mangetout) and @leafy greens{100%g}(spinach, pak choi), keeping each group in its own pile.

Finely chop @garlic{2%cloves} and @ginger{15%g} together. In a #small bowl{}, mix @soy sauce{2%tbsp}, @water{2%tbsp}, @cornflour{1%tsp} and @sugar{1%tsp}. Put everything next to the stove.

> Why: the cooking goes quickly, and there is no time to cut anything
> once it starts.
-- source: "How to Stir-Fry Without a Wok", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-10-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7725-%20how-to-stir-fry-without-a-wok

Heat half of the @oil{2%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) in a #large frying pan{} over high heat until it just starts to smoke. Add half the protein in one layer, leave it for ~{1%minute} to brown, then stir for about a minute more, until just cooked through. Tip it into a #bowl{} and cook the second half the same way.

> Why batches: a crowded pan makes the protein steam, not sear. In
> lesson 1.2 steaming was the point, because it collapses mushrooms.
> Here it is what you are avoiding.

> Science: this is the latent heat from lesson 1.2. Every gram of water
> that leaves the food as steam takes about 2,256 J with it, and water
> cannot pass 100 °C until it has vaporised. Put in too much food at
> once and the pan's heat goes into making steam: the surface stays
> near 100 °C, below the temperatures at which browning runs fast. Look up: latent heat
> of vaporization, Maillard reaction.
-- source: OpenStax, University Physics Volume 2, 2016, sections 1.4 (specific heat of water 4186 J per kg per °C) and 1.5 (latent heat of vaporization 2256 kJ/kg; temperature constant during the phase change), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes ; McGee, "High Temperatures and Dry Cooking Methods" (see lesson 1.1)

Add the rest of the oil to the pan. Add the hard vegetables and stir-fry for ~{2%minutes}. Add the soft vegetables and stir-fry for ~{2%minutes} more, then the leafy greens for ~{30%seconds}, until the greens have just wilted and the hard vegetables are tender but still have bite.

> Why in stages: each group needs a different time, and adding them in
> order means they all finish together.

Push the vegetables to the edge of the pan. Put the garlic and ginger in the middle and mash them into the pan for ~{30%seconds}, until you can smell them.

Return the protein to the pan. Stir the sauce, pour it in, and stir for ~{1%minute}, until it has thickened and coats everything. Serve at once, with rice if you like.

> Science: the sauce thickens by starch gelatinization. Heated in water
> above about 52 °C, starch granules swell as hydrogen bonds break,
> lose their crystalline order and release amylose, a linear chain that
> makes up 20 to 30% of the starch; the released amylose is what raises
> the viscosity. Cold, the cornflour only disperses, and the thickening
> starts when the sauce heats up. Look up: starch gelatinization,
> amylose, amylopectin.
-- source: Bashir and Aggarwal, "Physicochemical, structural and functional properties of native and irradiated starch: a review", Journal of Food Science and Technology 56(2):513-523, 2019, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13197-018-3530-2

> Next time, without the recipe: everything cut and next to the stove,
> pan hot, protein in batches and out, vegetables in order from hardest
> to softest, garlic and ginger in the middle, sauce last.
